DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260917T200000
UID:6a68e5507b79214226abe9d8@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This month's feature:\nPast Lives\nDescription: Nora and Hae 
 Sung\, two deeply connected childhood friends\,                           
        are wrest apart after Nora's family emigrates from South Korea. Two
  decades later\,                                      they are reunited in
  New York for one fateful week as they confront notions of                
                               destiny\, love\, and the choices that make a
  life\, in this heartrending modern                                       
    romance.\nThis film is rated PG-13. 106 minutes. Drop in. All are welco
 me!
LOCATION:Palmerston\, Downstairs theatre
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a68e5507b79214226abe9d8
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Movie Screening: Rochdale: Dream Tower
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260827T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260827T151500
UID:69ea39fb213814b1b524d47c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Dream Tower\n(1994\, Dir. Ron Mann) tells the tale of Rochdale
  College from its idealistic conception to drug addled demise.  Rochdale
  College (1968-1975) was an anti-institution where idealism and hedonism c
 onverged. Born out of the 1960’s activism against the repressive tendenc
 ies of traditional top-down university administration\, it sought a more a
 uthentic\, anti-hierarchal mode of learning combined with cooperative livi
 ng. It attracted students eager to learn and hippies experimenting with co
 untercultural ways of being. The mix was eventually subsumed by a bikers i
 nto North America's largest open drug market before it was closed for good
 .
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall\, 1st floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69ea39fb213814b1b524d47c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dystopia Nonfiction Book Club: The Crisis of Canadian Democracy
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260914T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260914T153000
UID:6a5f72a5f4e5db3d00c32d57@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us each month to discuss issues in politics and society\,
  and explore how powerful forces are exploiting our world and dystopian im
 aginings are becoming present-day realities.\nSeptember’s book is\nThe C
 risis of Canadian Democracy\nby journalist Andrew Coyne. Copies of the bo
 ok are available in print and eBook format. Book club attendees are encou
 raged to read the book in advance of the meeting.\nThe Crisis of Canadian
  Democracy "dismantles the comforting myths Canadians tell themselves abou
 t their political system\, revealing a parliamentary structure eroded by u
 naccountable leaders\, disempowered MPs\, manipulated elections\, and syst
 emic dysfunction. [The book] is both a wake-up call and a call to action\
 , offering compelling solutions to restore genuine self-government to Cana
 dian politics." Andrew Coyne warns Canadians that "as Parliament's legitim
 acy declines\, people look to alternate sources of representation... In ti
 me\, as we have seen south of the border\, the discrediting of democratic 
 institutions can begin to nourish a desire for autocracy."\nDrop in. No re
 gistration required.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Lower Level\, Room B1
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5f72a5f4e5db3d00c32d57
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Networking Meetup
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260829T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260829T163000
UID:6a566791fb418a28009ae31a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Connect with fellow Black entrepreneurs in your community. Bri
 ng your pitch\, bring your business cards\, and come ready to make real co
 nnections. This isn't small talk - it's purposeful networking designed to 
 open doors.\nPlease note:  Registered participant spaces will be held for 
 15 minutes after which drop-ins will be allowed.\nMeet the Resident:\nVivi
 an Kaye is a serial entrepreneur and business mentor who bootstrapped Kink
 yCurlyYaki to seven figures without outside funding. Over 15 years\, she h
 as mentored 300+ entrepreneurs\, recently facilitating workshops for Found
 Hers Innovation Labs and Humber College's Entrepreneurship Program.\nFeatu
 red in The New York Times\, Toronto Star\, and on CityTV's Cityline\, Vivi
 an has partnered with brands including Shopify\, Amazon\, and American Exp
 ress Canada. Her direct\, practical approach helps early-stage Black entre
 preneurs navigate the realities of building sustainable businesses without
  traditional support systems.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe persona
 l information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library unde
 r the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The infor
 mation will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and
  evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information can 
 be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 7
 89 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Mount Dennis\, Meeting Room 2
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a566791fb418a28009ae31a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
