BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Farzana Islam
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260808T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260808T235959
UID:6a6e59f124cc9f017dda3d88@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of August\, the works of artist Farzana I
 sland are on display at Albion Branch.\nFarzana Islam is a Bangladeshi Ca
 nadian artist and illustrator based in Toronto. She tells stories that don
 ’t always get the spotlight\, aiming to leave a\nfeeling that stays with
  people. From making webcomics as a teen and tattoo apprenticeships to stu
 dying illustration at Sheridan College\, she now\nworks across illustratio
 n\, animation\, and 3D\, bringing untold perspectives to life in human\, m
 eaningful ways.\n"Farzana’s work is rooted in her experience of entering
  the Canadian school system as an immigrant child\, carrying languages and
  stories that didn’t always fit. Shaped by Scarborough’s vibrant dive
 rsity\, her art brings to light how young people carve out belonging withi
 n educational and cultural landscapes. Through her work with youth and he
 r lens as a queer Muslim artist\, she explores identity\, literacy\, and a
 mplifies voices often overlooked but quietly shared across communities."\n
 The exhibit can be viewed during Albion branch's open hours. Please check 
 the\nAlbion branch\npage before you visit.\nFree. All are welcome.\nPhoto 
 credit: Farzana Islam\,\nThinking Back\, Watercolour and Coloured Pencil\,
  61 * 91.
LOCATION:Albion
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a6e59f124cc9f017dda3d88
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ask The Founder: Real Business Lessons & Community Q&A
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260825T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260825T200000
UID:6a5a694e82c03c5c17d8b038@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:What does entrepreneurship really look like behind the scenes?
 \nJoin Entrepreneur in Residence Melanie Cruickshank (Founder of da lish c
 osmetics)\, Stacey Davis (Founder of LOVEFRESH) and Sharon Smyl (Founder o
 f Collected Joy) for an open and honest conversation about the realities 
 of building a business.\nEntrepreneur in Residence Melanie Cruickshank\, F
 ounder of da lish cosmetics\nMelanie Cruickshank is a founder and brand-si
 de operator with over 15 years of experience building\, launching\, and sc
 aling beauty and personal care brands. As the founder of da lish cosmetics
 \, she led everything from product development and retail strategy to e-co
 mmerce growth and national partnerships. Known for her hands-on\, no-nonse
 nse approach\, Melanie brings a founder’s accountability and a deep unde
 rstanding of what it takes to build brands that perform in market. Melani
 e will share lessons learned\, challenges overcome\, unexpected pivots\, a
 nd practical insights from building and growing a consumer brand.\nStacey 
 Davis\, Founder of LOVEFRESH\nStacey Davis is the visionary entrepreneur 
 and creative mind behind Toronto-based all-natural body care brand LOVEFRE
 SH\, a company she was destined to helm. Prior to founding LOVEFRESH\, Sta
 cey nurtured dreams of becoming an interior designer. However\, it was her
  encounter with the world of scents that set her on an unexpected journey.
  It all begins with a spark – the earliest iteration of LOVEFRESH was i
 gnited during a workshop on crafting cold processed soap. Stacey fell in l
 ove with the art of scent blending\, and she quickly realized that her new
 found passion had potential to bloom into a business venture. LOVEFRESH wa
 s born in 2007\, expanding beyond soap to offering a full collection of bo
 dy care products & home essentials.\nSharon Smyl\, Founder of Collected Jo
 y\n"My first job was in retail at the Lollipop Shop in St. Paul\, Alberta 
 when I was 13 years old.  I didn't realize it at the time that that job wa
 s going to teach me the core tenets of running my own business - customer 
 service applies to every job you have\, follow your talent because you'll 
  find passion in the things you're really good at and it's 100% ok to pivo
 t and change throughout your career. I first opened up Collected Joy in O
 ctober of 2014 after 15+ years in the marketing and advertising world and 
 prior to that\, 8 years in non-profit. With Collected Joy\, I knew I want
 ed to create a welcoming space filled with laughter and connections with a
  focus on selling mostly small-batch\, Canadian-made designs due to the we
 alth of creativity that exists in this country.  And I knew I wanted to bu
 ild myself a job that I was going to love doing every single day."\nPartic
 ipants are encouraged to bring their questions\, share their own experienc
 es\, and engage in a community discussion focused on the realities of entr
 epreneurship. Whether you're just getting started or already running a bus
 iness\, this session offers an opportunity to learn from real-world experi
 ences and reflect on your own entrepreneurial journey.\nAll sessions are i
 nteractive and participants are encouraged to bring questions related to t
 heir own business or entrepreneurial goals.\nThis program is part of Toron
 to Public Library’s\nEntrepreneur in Residence\nprogram series with a fo
 cus on women entrepreneurs\, designed to support women as they explore ent
 repreneurship and build practical small business skills. Participants will
  learn about key topics for starting and growing a business\, with guidanc
 e from an experienced entrepreneur in residence. For more information and 
 a full list of programs\, visit\ntpl.ca/eir\n.
LOCATION:S. Walter Stewart\, Meeting Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5a694e82c03c5c17d8b038
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
