BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Creative Writing Club: Positive Poetry and Insightful Literature w
 ith Farazaneh Haratyan
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260807T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260807T200000
UID:6a56af7bc7e02e3d006928be@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:What happens when the art of the story meets the science of th
 e mind?\nDr. Farzaneh Haratyan\nis a psychologist in supervised practice w
 ith the College of Psychologists and Behavioural Analysts of Ontario (CPBA
 O) and an associate professor who has taught across international higher e
 ducation settings. With dual doctorates bridging literature and psychology
 \, her work explores how expressive writing\, poetry\, and narrative refra
 ming foster resilience\, emotional regulation\, and optimal wellbeing. Her
  peer-reviewed research and poetry—including pieces published in the\nJo
 urnal of Poetry Therapy\nand\nEnglish in Education\n—highlight the thera
 peutic power of transforming inner landscapes through metaphor and creativ
 e agency:\n"Do not fear the map drawn in embers\;\nit only marks where the
  ground learned to hold light."\n— Dr. Farzaneh Haratyan\, from\n"Cartog
 raphy of Fire and Flame: A Testament"\n(\nJournal of English in Education\
 , 2026\n)\nIn an era where many seek meaningful tools to navigate mental h
 ealth challenges and daily stresses\, Dr. Haratyan invites you to step int
 o a supportive\, restorative space. Join a unique workshop led by a clinic
 ian and literary scholar to experience firsthand how the simple act of put
 ting pen to paper can heal\, uplift\, and rewrite your personal story.\n"W
 hen we put our felt experiences into rhythm and metaphor\, we move from pa
 ssively enduring our emotions to actively shaping them. Poetry gives us th
 e pen to rewrite how a story ends within us."\n— Dr. Farzaneh Haratyan\,
 \n“Poetic Re-Authoring as Emotional Regulation”\n(2026)\nInformation C
 ollection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by t
 he Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\
 , 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and progr
 am registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library
  handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerli
 ne@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Malvern
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a56af7bc7e02e3d006928be
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
