BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Justin Gray:  Building a Life in Music: Observations from the Stud
 io\, Stage\, and Classroom
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260905T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260905T160000
UID:6a6a2e8be9de6536001f10c6@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:T.U. Jazz Fest 2026 Masterclass Series\nJustin Gray is a 2026 
 Grammy award-wining producer and a Juno award-winning bassist\, composer\,
  engineer\, producer\, and educator based in Toronto\, ON. As a double bas
 sist\, electric bassist\, and Bass Veena player\, Justin performs in a var
 iety of traditional and contemporary music ensembles from around the world
 . Justin is on faculty at Humber College in Toronto\, where he teaches au
 dio production\, performance\, and composition.  As a producer\, mix engin
 eer and mastering engineer\, Justin has worked with artists from around th
 e world in a wide range of musical styles.\nOver his career\, Justin has r
 eceived several awards and distinctions\, including 2020 CFMA Nominee (Bes
 t World Album)\, 2018 CFMA Nominee (Best Solo Artist)\, 2017 Canadian Scre
 en Award (Co-Producer of Best Original Song)\, 2015 Emerging Jazz Artist A
 ward (Toronto Arts Foundation) and 2015 CFMA Nomination (Best World Album)
 .\nNo advance registration required. All ages welcome. For more informatio
 n\, please call the Languages\, Literature & Fine Arts Department at 416-
 395-5639.\nIn partnership with the Emerging Artists Association
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Auditorium\,  2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a6a2e8be9de6536001f10c6
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Farzana Islam
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260825T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260825T235959
UID:6a6e59f124cc9f017dda3d99@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of August\, the works of artist Farzana I
 sland are on display at Albion Branch.\nFarzana Islam is a Bangladeshi Ca
 nadian artist and illustrator based in Toronto. She tells stories that don
 ’t always get the spotlight\, aiming to leave a\nfeeling that stays with
  people. From making webcomics as a teen and tattoo apprenticeships to stu
 dying illustration at Sheridan College\, she now\nworks across illustratio
 n\, animation\, and 3D\, bringing untold perspectives to life in human\, m
 eaningful ways.\n"Farzana’s work is rooted in her experience of entering
  the Canadian school system as an immigrant child\, carrying languages and
  stories that didn’t always fit. Shaped by Scarborough’s vibrant dive
 rsity\, her art brings to light how young people carve out belonging withi
 n educational and cultural landscapes. Through her work with youth and he
 r lens as a queer Muslim artist\, she explores identity\, literacy\, and a
 mplifies voices often overlooked but quietly shared across communities."\n
 The exhibit can be viewed during Albion branch's open hours. Please check 
 the\nAlbion branch\npage before you visit.\nFree. All are welcome.\nPhoto 
 credit: Farzana Islam\,\nThinking Back\, Watercolour and Coloured Pencil\,
  61 * 91.
LOCATION:Albion
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a6e59f124cc9f017dda3d99
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum Art Heist
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260807T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260807T200000
UID:6a5f84670e562e28009b58eb@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:On March 18\, 1990\, two men dressed as police officers broke 
 into the Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum in Boston and stole 13 masterpiec
 es worth $500M\, including five Degas\, three Rembrandts\, and a Vermeer.\
 nThis seminar will illuminate who Isabella Stewart Gardner was\, how she a
 massed thousands of pieces of art\, who may have perpetrated the heist\, t
 he implications of art theft\, and other fascinating facts about the large
 st unsolved art heist in history.\nPresented by author and artist Jerry Le
 vy.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Hosted in the discussion room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5f84670e562e28009b58eb
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Cyanotype Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260807T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260807T113000
UID:6a64c7200e562e28009c5687@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:What materials and processes existed before Adobe Photoshop? J
 oin us for a hands-on analog image making workshop to create a Cyanotype. 
 The Cyanotype is a photographic printing technique sensitive to a limited 
 near ultraviolet and blue light spectrum. It involves layering objects and
  transparencies. All are welcome!\nWe will supply all materials\, but feel
  free to bring an object or 2D silhouette for a contact print.\nInformatio
 n Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected b
 y the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(
 a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and pr
 ogram registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the libr
 ary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswe
 rline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a64c7200e562e28009c5687
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
