BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Building Your Own Website for Small Business
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261007T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261007T190000
UID:6a6cbd76d23de82955c9b556@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:In today's world\, having a personal website is essential for 
 many reasons and especially to support careers and small businesses. Learn
  how to use WordPress to build your own e-commerce or small business websi
 te. This session workshop will cover everything from the basic fundamental
 s and tools of WordPress all the way to the platform's advanced features t
 hat support promoting and running a small business.\nPlease arrive a few m
 inutes early.\nPlease cancel as soon as possible if you are no longer avai
 lable to attend.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information
  on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public 
 Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will o
 nly be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. 
 Questions about how the library handles your information can be directed t
 o Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\
 , Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Albert Campbell\, Learning Centre\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a6cbd76d23de82955c9b556
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Mickey Arts Exhibition
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260801T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260831T235959
UID:6a6a4cb8e9de6536001f1cf0@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of August\, the works of artist Michael A
 lemayehu are on display at\nDeer Park branch\n.\nMichael Alemayehu is a se
 lf-taught Ethiopian artist based in Canada. Through graphite and colored p
 encils\, he creates realistic portraits and wildlife art. His work reflec
 ts his heritage and passion for detail\, inviting viewers to see the beaut
 y of Africa and beyond.\n"Art has always been my language of expression an
 d connection. Inspired by my Ethiopian roots\, I use realism to honor cult
 ure\, identity\, and nature. With every drawing\, I aim to spark fascinat
 ion and share stories that celebrate beauty and tradition."\nThis exhibit 
 is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibit Space\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provi
 des space for local artists to exhibit their work at locations across the 
 city. These exhibits aim to reflect the diverse cultural interests of the 
 city and its neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be viewed during Deer Park's
  open hours. Please check the\nDeer Park branch\npage before your visit. 
 The branch will be closed on August 2nd and 3rd.\nFree. All are welcome.\n
 Image credit: Artist: Michael Alemayehu\nTitle of Piece: Culture in Motion
 \nMedium: Coloured pencil on bristol paper\nSize/Dimensions: Extra Large
LOCATION:Deer Park\, Main Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a6a4cb8e9de6536001f1cf0
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Between the Lines Bookclub
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260821T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260821T200000
UID:6962837f91e4576f847bd102@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Become a page-turner by joining the Between the Lines Book Clu
 b! Here\, we embark on a new chapter with every read\, diving into a world
  of endless possibilities. From gripping thrillers to heartwarming coming-
 of-age tales\, or a sprinkle of fantasy adventures\, there's something for
  everyone. Whether you're a die-hard fan of one genre or love mixing thing
 s up\, you'll find a place here to discuss\, share\, and connect with fell
 ow readers. Ready to explore different worlds and discover your next favor
 ite book? Join us and let's turn the page together!\nFriday July 17\n: Whe
 re the Crawdad Sings by Delia Owens\nWhere the Crawdads Sing\nis a beauti
 fully written novel that blends mystery\, coming-of-age\, and romance. Set
  in the marshes of North Carolina\, it follows Kya Clark\, a young girl wh
 o grows up isolated from society and finds comfort in the natural world. A
 s the story unfolds\, themes of loneliness\, resilience\, love\, and belon
 ging intertwine with the investigation of a local man's death. It's a comp
 elling read that invites discussion about human nature\, prejudice\, and o
 ur connection to the world around us.\nIf you need help getting a copy of 
 the book or have any questions\, please contact us at sastaff@tpl.ca or 41
 6-393-7653. Copies of the monthly titles are available at the circulation 
 desk.
LOCATION:Sanderson\, Main Floor Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6962837f91e4576f847bd102
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
